WEBVTT

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:26.640
daydreaming, Connie. You'll be late for school. Connie, wake up. Who are you? Well, I'm not

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.500
Bridie Murphy. When did all this take place?

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:37.740
I was still teaching at Madison then, and we were living in your house on Carroll Avenue.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.170
The day started very simply. You were fixing breakfast for me that morning.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.310
Connie! Oh, Connie, are you awake?

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.570
No, Aunt Mrs. Davis!

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:59.800
You've got to get dressed. Your food is getting cold.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:11.140
I put the coffee, no, it's in the percolator.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.890
Let's see what else, oh, the toast, oh, Connie, what kind of toast do you want?

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.230
White or rye?

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.530
You startled me so, dear, I didn't hear you coming in.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.900
my tomato juice warm, Mrs. Davis.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.320
Oh, you're welcome, dear.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.270
How'd you sleep last night?

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:37.930
Pretty well, thanks.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.600
I had a dream about Mr. Boynton.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:41.860
What happened?

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.170
Like always, nothing.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.470
You know, I can't understand that man

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:50.450
and him a biology teacher.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.030
There's a big difference between teaching it

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:54.270
and learning it, I guess.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.040
How long have you two known each other, anyway?

00:01:58.920 --> 00:01:59.580
Let's see.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.200
We started dating the week he got his pet frog, McDougall.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.300
That was over four years ago.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.420
You mean that you've been going steady for the past four years?

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:10.720
That's right, Mrs. Davis.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.260
I've been going steady with Mr. Boynton,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.970
and he's been going steady with McDougall.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.660
I'll get it.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.760
Oh, that must be Walter Dent to take me to school.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.280
I hope he's had the top put up on that popped-up jalopy of his.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.410
Good morning, Miss Brooks.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.220
It is a glorious morning.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.580
An appetizingly delicious morning.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.360
Yeah, well, don't wrap it up.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.340
I'll eat it here.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.400
Sit down, Walter.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.320
Oh, thank you.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.570
Are you still eating breakfast?

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:49.190
Uh-huh.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.110
Yeah, I had my breakfast an hour ago.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.790
Oh, that's too bad.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.840
I thought I might fix you a few waffles, dear.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.220
Oh, no, thanks.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.490
No, I couldn't eat any waffles, Mrs. Davis.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.660
Just a few eggs and some sausage and toast will be plenty.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.300
All right, dear.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.140
I'll pick you up in a jiffy.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.440
Gee, I feel wonderful today.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.580
You know, I'm so happy I can hardly eat.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.820
Would you pass a jelly, please?

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.130
You really are walking on the ceiling this morning, Walter.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.620
What's the source of all this gaiety?

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.320
Oh, the source is Harriet Conklin, Miss Brooks.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.140
I'm crazy about her.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.060
And what a fight we had yesterday.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.770
We almost came to blows.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:26.960
I see.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.420
You're crazy about her, so you almost came to blows.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.350
Well, you see, a person can't take another person for granted

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.330
if he or she is clobbering his or her brains out.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:39.090
Clobbering?

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.420
Don't you get it?

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.440
No, I didn't.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:48.100
The more the battle, the sweeter it is making up.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.620
To really pick fights just so you can make up later?

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.120
You should have heard the name she called me.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.640
Harriet called you names?

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.080
Well, she was just quoting her father.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:03:59.970
Her beloved principal...

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:08.640
me, you know. Mr. Conklin doesn't hate anyone, Walter. He just looks that way. Well, you see,

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:14.640
Miss Brooks, a good fight once in a while tends to make couples less sure of each other. I think

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.180
it'd help a lot if you had a good battle with Mr. Boynton. If I'm not getting too personal. You're

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.920
getting too personal. Oh, honestly, Walter, I never heard such adolescent gibberish. Now, if you'll

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.480
excuse me. Yeah, but you gotta do something to stir him up or he'll never make a move.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.610
I've just had a wonderful idea.

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.610
I'm going to pick a fight with Mr. Boyden.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:40.830
What?

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.280
If I don't stir him up some way, he'll never make a move.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.840
But if I can get him into a good, lively battle,

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.480
think of the fun we'll have making up.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.960
Sounds like a wonderful idea.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.320
When are you going to try it, Connie?

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.160
At noon today.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.280
I have a date with him in the school cafeteria.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.700
But remember, Mrs. Davis, this is absolutely confidential.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:04:59.960
I don't want you to make...

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:16.460
it to her soul. Oh, you can trust me. Yes, what, Walter? Miss Brooks is going to pick a fight with Mr. Boynton.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:24.930
They were cooing and gurgling at each other like a brace of punch-drunk doves.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.640
They were just making up after a little spat.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.020
You see, sir, there's nothing quite as sweet as the reconciliation after a lover's quarrel.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.390
Why, make a couple's pig fights just for the fun of making up.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.690
What repugnant theatrics.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.660
Although, now that you mention it,

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.090
Harriet's mother once started an argument like that with me.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.660
To stir up some emotional activity, she said.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.920
To think of it, when we did make up, it was rather wonderful.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:08.380
Make a note to call Mrs. Compton the first chance I get.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:09.730
I must call Mrs.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.290
Oh, it seems to be empty, sir.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:12.510
Here, I'll fill it for you.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:13.710
No, that's quite all right, Miss Brooks.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.450
Oh, it's no trouble at all, Mr. Compton.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:34.570
I should like it very much if you would leave my office.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.170
Wouldn't you like a blotter?

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.050
No.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.870
If you are not out of this office by the time I count three,

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:50.330
I shall end.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:15.470
Oh hello Miss Brooks. You're looking well today, I'll bet.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.620
I've been holding this table for us. It's right near the food counter.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.160
Well if you're going to be sarcastic just because I happen to be two or three minutes late,

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.080
perhaps I'd better sit by myself.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.320
I wasn't being sarcastic, Miss Brooks, I just said.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.020
There you go, you just said.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.300
I don't suppose you're at all interested in what I might have to say.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.840
Of course I am, Miss Brooks.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:40.680
What have you got to say?

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.590
I forget.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:48.010
Why did you sit so close to the theme table?

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.350
You always like to be near the food, Miss Brooks.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.670
Oh, that's right.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:52.990
Call me a glutton.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:59.290
The next thing you'll be saying is that I spit myself so I'm getting fat as a horse.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.360
I wouldn't say anything like that.

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:04.370
You might be getting a little bit clumped.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.320
What's the matter with you today, anyway?

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.520
Oh, of course. Blame it all on me.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.760
I come in here prepared to have a perfectly friendly lunch,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.680
and immediately you begin picking on me.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:18.900
I don't know how you can say a thing like that.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.620
You mean you don't know how a person in her right mind

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.660
could say a thing like that.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.160
That's what you really mean, isn't it?

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.560
I get it now. This is a rib.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.700
Say, you sure had me going for a minute there.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.590
I may have to bite him.

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.380
No lib, Mr. Boynton.

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:39.380
I don't feel the truth to you.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.720
Excuse me, folks, but I have an important message for Miss Brooks.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:45.110
Mr. Conklin wants to see you in his office rather immediately.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.130
How immediately?

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:50.990
Well, if memory serves me correctly, his exact words were on the double.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:52.140
Uh-oh.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.640
It's a shame you have to go now, Miss Brooks.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:56.860
Well, you haven't had anything to eat.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:58.830
Oh, tell you what.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:08:59.910
Well,

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.470
Why don't you have a few of these jelly beans?

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:02.670
Jelly beans?

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.370
Yes. I carry them with me all the time.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.890
The dextrose contained in them is one of the best sources of quick energy known to science.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:11.530
Here, take a few.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:13.630
Sweets to the sweet, they say.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.660
That is the last insult I will take from you today.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:19.620
Insult?

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.200
Pardon me for interrupting, Mr. Boynton, but as manager of the track team,

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.120
I called a special practice for after school.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.280
Well, for your information, Denton, I'm coach of the track team,

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.900
and I'm the only one entitled to call a practice.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.290
Mr. Boyne, you have to tell him I have to get so upset.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:33.350
Don't tell me what to do.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.220
I'm not strict enough with you kids.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:35.820
That's the trouble.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:36.740
Well, sure, you're fine.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:40.800
Great.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:42.410
With him, he fights.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:49.710
Yes, Miss Brooks, I did.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:53.490
I wanted to report to you the result of my adopting your suggestion.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:54.940
My suggestion?

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:55.640
Yes.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.710
Yes, acting on your conversational tip of a few moments ago,

00:09:58.870 --> 00:09:59.970
I called my wife.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.940
Martha and started an argument with her.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.160
Really, Mr. Conklin? Do you think it worked?

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.320
I think it did, yes.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:11.700
Yes?

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.090
Martha has left me!

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:14.930
Oh!

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:21.740
Well, I thought I'd gone at it a bit strongly on my first call,

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.020
so after a few moments I rang my home again.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:24.400
And?

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:27.820
The housekeeper informed me that Mrs. Conklin had left the house with a valise.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:28.660
With a valise?

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.980
The same thing happened the last time...

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.140
She took off for her mother's home in Montrose like a wounded buffalo.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.040
You will stay in my office, Miss Brooks.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.740
I'll try to head her off at the railroad station.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:40.560
But, sir, my pleasure.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.420
Put my daughter Harriet in charge as monitor.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:43.940
You must stay here till I get back.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:47.040
Well, I'm terribly sorry it worked out like this, Mr. Conklin,

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:49.540
but I must say you're showing remarkable self-control.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:50.800
Thank you.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.750
There's no time for recrimination now.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:57.660
However, upon my return to this institution, I shall have...

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:27.760
door. Principal speaking. Hello, Miss Brooke. This is Mrs. Conklin. Just a moment, please.

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:36.070
Are you shocked?

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.010
I'm having one of my valises repaired.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:41.610
Would you call Mr. Conklin to the phone, please?

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.410
I'm afraid my voice isn't loud enough for that.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.190
He's at the railroad station.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.560
The railroad station?

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:49.360
What's he doing there?

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:51.430
Well, he thought you were peeved at him.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:53.880
So when your housekeeper said you'd left with a valise,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.900
he presumed something drastic had happened.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:56.990
Draft?

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.360
Like the time you were wounded in Buffalo.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.180
On your way to your mother's house in Montrose.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:10.710
I feel very much to blame for this whole thing, Mrs. Conklin,

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:15.200
because I suggested that a good quarrel now and then was very stimulating.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:17.010
You're so right, Miss Brooks.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.270
A little emotional activity is good for any family.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.870
Why, even the bees have to stop making honey once in a while.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:28.330
Yes, but I started the activity in the wrong hive.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:37.000
However, it's a bad idea to let Mr. Conklin cool his heels in that railroad station for a while.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.470
That's just what I'll do.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:43.020
Say, Miss Brooks, why don't you drop by the house after school?

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:45.080
We'll have a nice little bull session.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:47.260
Oh, he won't be back by then.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.470
That is, I'd love to, Mrs. Conklin. Thank you.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:53.270
See you this afternoon, then. Goodbye.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:53.910
Goodbye.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.370
Miss Brooks, I'm surprised at you.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:12:59.490
Mr. Boynton.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.120
I came in to see Mr. Conklin, and I couldn't help overhearing your conversation with Mrs. Conklin.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.100
Well, I'd like one of those jelly beans now, Mr. Boynton.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.660
Frankly, I never dreamed you'd be a party to such a nefarious plot.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:17.110
For the first time in our association, Miss Brooks, I'm genuinely angry at you.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:18.060
You are?

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.560
I certainly am.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:26.180
You want to make something of it, frog boy?

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:33.120
Frog boy.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:37.320
Go to the pond and dunk your head under a lily pad.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:42.340
So, you've been spying on me after school.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.600
Hiya, Father Bunsen burner.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:53.740
When I think about principal wandering around the railroad station searching vainly for a wife who isn't there,

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:55.030
I'm appalled.

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:57.610
Poor Mr. Conklin must be beside himself.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:59.970
Please, Mr. Boynton, not even Mr. Conklin.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.840
could stand another Mr. Conklin.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:04.560
Your levity leaves me cold.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:05.520
Good day, Miss Brooks.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:07.080
Good day, Mr. Boynton.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.430
Miss Brooks, after 9 p.m.

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:15.870
and still no word from Osgood,

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:17.940
something's happened to him.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.380
It isn't like Danny to be gone this long without calling.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.020
Oh, I'm sure he'll turn up at any minute, Harriet.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.090
You know the story of the proverbial bad penny.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:28.560
Now, I guess I shouldn't tell that one now.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:37.300
the bright side of things i suppose you're right miss brooks would you like some more coffee no

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:45.720
thanks harriet i never drink more than 14 cups how'd you mr boynton no thank you i can't help

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.020
thinking about that story in the papers the other day about the college professor who was found

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:57.800
wandering around with amnesia his wife had left him too leave your father harriet he only thought i did

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.980
If only Daddy hadn't taken Walter Denton's...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.380
to drive to the station and hardly control that hopped up jalopy and this is dreadful again

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:15.790
oh now take it easy mrs conklin how can i take it easy you feel if you thought you were never

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:29.590
going to see osgood again a cup of coffee i'm sure everything will turn out fine mrs conklin

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.520
How can you say that, Mr. Boynton?

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:32.900
The man's missing, isn't he?

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.720
You heard about the professor who wandered off with amnesia.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.440
Why should Mr. Conklin have amnesia?

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:39.480
Why shouldn't he?

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:43.140
Don't you two start quarreling.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:44.840
We already have quarreled.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:46.710
Forget it.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:47.830
Maybe it's Osgood.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:51.140
Come in.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:51.800
Good evening.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:55.080
Good evening, Harriet.

